---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-justuno';
const title = 'Privy vs Justuno for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Privy capture and email/SMS sending with Justuno onsite capture and personalization: permissions, integrations, merchant tests and total operating costs.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Privy send email and SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes. Privy advertises capture, email and SMS campaigns and automations. Confirm your plan, sender approval, recipient geography and required workflow in your account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Justuno replace an SMS sender?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Justuno for onsite capture, targeting and personalization connected to your sending tools. An integration or onsite journey is not evidence of native email or SMS dispatch."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Privy a useful fit?",
    "answer": "Privy is a candidate when a team wants capture and email/SMS sending together and its account passes the campaign, permission and suppression tests."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is Justuno a useful fit?",
    "answer": "Justuno is a candidate when onsite targeting and offer experiments matter and the chosen email/SMS integration preserves the fields and permission evidence your sender needs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I add Sequenzy or Postscript?",
    "answer": "No. Keep a working sender when it meets the job. Consider another email or SMS tool only for an identified gap, with a verified connector and a named owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a phone number establish SMS permission?",
    "answer": "No. Keep channel, purpose, source, timestamp and disclosure version separately from identity. Visitor identification and email signup do not establish promotional SMS permission."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits work?",
    "answer": "Use authoritative Shopify order events to stop the relevant promotion, retire queued messages and reconcile delayed events before release. Test each sender rather than assuming capture rules control its queue."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should duplicate events be handled?",
    "answer": "Use stable event and campaign identifiers, a send ledger and bounded retries. Quarantine conflicting identities or missing permission instead of enrolling a profile again."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which option costs less overall?",
    "answer": "Compare matched eligible cohorts and the same time window, including capture, senders, integration, messages, labor, support and migration costs. The hypothetical worksheet below is not a vendor quote or performance forecast."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Privy vs Justuno</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Capture and campaign operations</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Justuno for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Privy combines capture with email and SMS sending. Justuno focuses on onsite capture, targeting and personalization connected to marketing tools. Choose around the job your team needs to improve and prove the path from signup to an eligible message.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Capture and sending', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Candidate when its combined email/SMS workflow meets your account requirements' },
          { label: 'Onsite experiments', tool: 'Justuno', reason: 'Candidate when targeting and personalization justify a verified sender integration' },
          { label: 'Existing sender', tool: 'Keep if suitable', reason: 'Add or replace a tool only for a demonstrated workflow gap' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What the products own</h2>
        <p>Reviewed November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/pricing">Privy describes</a> displays, email/SMS campaigns and automations, plus a displays-only option. <a class="link" href="https://www.justuno.com/">Justuno describes</a> popups, behavioral targeting, visitor identification and connections to email, SMS and commerce tools. Those connections do not establish native dispatch or prove that every field travels in both directions. Confirm plan access, regional availability and sender approval before a pilot; no fixed price or conversion claim is used here.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy candidate</th><th>Justuno candidate</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Capture</td><td>Displays with combined sending or a capture-only configuration</td><td>Onsite offers and targeting with a selected destination</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Email and SMS</td><td>Test its native campaign and automation path where enabled</td><td>Identify the actual sender and verify the integration path</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Personalization</td><td>Test required captured fields and campaign branches</td><td>Test visitor rules, offer variants and destination field mapping</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Operating ownership</td><td>One platform can reduce handoffs if the workflow passes</td><td>An onsite specialist can retain an existing sender if the handoff passes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h2>Capture-to-sender contract</h2>
        <p>Name the capture owner, Shopify data owner and sending owner, even if one person fills all three roles. For each form map source/form ID, disclosure version, timestamp, channel, purpose, email, normalized phone, customer ID, selected interests and destination list or custom field. Check which connector actually supports each field, its direction, latency and failure behavior. An unmapped field must not default to permission.</p>
        <p>A Privy pilot can use its enabled native email/SMS path or a verified external destination. A Justuno pilot should name the connected email and SMS sender and prove each enrollment. Keep email and SMS purpose permissions independent. Visitor identification is an identity signal, not permission; quarantine conflicting phone/email merges and do not attach a gift recipient to the purchaser. Preserve opt-outs and disclosure evidence through imports and account changes.</p>
        <p>Use Shopify orders and inventory as agreed sources of truth. Reconcile late, duplicate, cancelled and refunded events. Define which purchases end which promotions, then check exits before enqueue and immediately before release. Cancel queued messages at every active sender; changing a capture form alone does not retire a scheduled send. Use a stable event/campaign/recipient key and send ledger to prevent retries from producing duplicate invitations.</p>
        <h2>Fourteen proposed merchant acceptance fixtures</h2>
        <p>All merchants below are fictional proposed test fixtures, not customers, measured outcomes or endorsements. Each preserves a different merchant job. Privy is a positive candidate if its combined capture and sending path passes the relevant fixture. Justuno is a positive candidate if its onsite rules and verified destination achieve the same result. Neither conditional fit guarantees a particular feature in your account.</p>
        <h3>Festival Threads Co: tour-city scheduling</h3>
        <p>Capture an explicitly selected city and timezone; do not infer them from a venue page. Propose a three-text city cap. Replay a daylight-saving boundary and reject recipients with unresolved timezone.</p>
        <h3>Ridge Run Coffee: subscription exclusions</h3>
        <p>Combine subscription status with authoritative orders. An active subscriber must exit the flash offer; replay a delayed subscription event and retire an already queued promotion.</p>
        <h3>Cipher Audio: full-price launch education</h3>
        <p>Separate product education email from launch SMS permission. Compare a no-discount holdout over the same window; a page visit alone must not enroll an SMS launch recipient.</p>
        <h3>Patchwork Denim: one-of-one style matching</h3>
        <p>Map a chosen style to an available SKU. Sell the last piece before dispatch and require the sender to cancel the stale offer rather than reuse a cached inventory count.</p>
        <h3>Arena Energy: collaboration offer boundaries</h3>
        <p>Keep a flavor preference separate from an email-only shaker incentive. Verify that an SMS link does not accidentally grant or promise that email offer.</p>
        <h3>Loom &amp; Frame: edition scarcity</h3>
        <p>Use a documented edition total and current inventory before any scarcity claim. Test the proposed halfway threshold against a returned order; no invented sell-through result is implied.</p>
        <h3>Driftwood Surf Co: seasonal restock waiting</h3>
        <p>Capture size and restock purpose separately from general promotion permission. An old waitlist request must not silently authorize a new seasonal broadcast.</p>
        <h3>Noir Collective: VIP early access</h3>
        <p>Propose a two-hour access window, verify VIP status from the agreed customer record and test the link with a non-VIP account. Queue release requires an inventory and eligibility recheck.</p>
        <h3>SoleDrop ATX: size-run waitlist priority</h3>
        <p>Preserve enrollment time and size without promising an allocation. Replay duplicate webhook deliveries and require one queue entry per person, release and size.</p>
        <h3>Vinyl Vault Records: collector eligibility</h3>
        <p>Define collector value from net settled orders after returns rather than a captured self-description. Correct a refunded order before generating the next access audience.</p>
        <h3>Bloom &amp; Wick: gift shipping deadlines</h3>
        <p>Collect gifting intent without merging the gift recipient into the buyer profile. Test destination-specific cutoffs and cancel the deadline promotion after purchase.</p>
        <h3>Peak Trail Gear: clearance offer restraint</h3>
        <p>Define a clearance segment and a full-price holdout before approval. Record discount redemptions once in net sales; do not claim an incentive improved margin without measurement.</p>
        <h3>Sauce Lab: batch inventory alerts</h3>
        <p>Propose a launch and one inventory alert for a named batch. Test an inventory feed outage: pause scarcity messaging instead of repeating the last known quantity.</p>
        <h3>Ember Ceramics: small-batch fulfillment order</h3>
        <p>Keep maker-story email separate from access SMS. Preserve the approved waitlist order during a sender handoff and prove that rollback does not create a second access invitation.</p>
        <h2>Approvals, handoff and rollback</h2>
        <p>Before release, the merchandising owner approves inventory and offer claims; the channel owner approves audience, frequency and purchase exits; the permission owner reviews disclosure, purpose and applicable recipient rules. Configure quiet hours and reply handling with the actual sender, including unknown timezones and opt-out propagation. These are operating checks, not a legal compliance guarantee.</p>
        <p>During handoff, freeze the old campaign, export suppression and enrollment evidence, record queued IDs, retire old queued jobs and reconcile in-flight messages before activating the new path. Rehearse missing permissions, delayed orders, connector outages and retry duplication. Keep failed records out of sends until repaired. Sign off on a test ledger showing exactly one authorized message or the expected suppression.</p>
        <p>For rollback, pause the new capture enrollment and sender queues, reconcile the send ledger and preserve new opt-outs. Restore the approved form and mappings, then release only records that remain eligible; never replay the whole imported list. Assign a rollback owner and a stop threshold for unexplained sends, identity conflicts or purchase-exit failures.</p>
        <h2>Matched hypothetical total operating economics</h2>
        <p>Compare the same 1,000 eligible customers, geography, offer, attribution window and holdout method across a Privy configuration and a Justuno-plus-sender configuration. These invented amounts illustrate a worksheet, not prices or projected results. Suppose each test produces $4,000 incremental net sales after discounts and returns, less $1,600 goods and fulfillment and $200 payment/support cost: $2,200 before tooling and operations. Do not subtract discounts or returns again.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Hypothetical period cost</th><th>Privy configuration</th><th>Justuno + sender configuration</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Capture/platform and email/SMS sender</td><td>$300</td><td>$450</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Message/carrier usage</td><td>$100</td><td>$100</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Integration monitoring and repair</td><td>$100</td><td>$200</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Campaign and support labor</td><td>$400</td><td>$300</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Setup/migration allocated to the same period</td><td>$200</td><td>$150</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Total operating cost</td><td>$1,100</td><td>$1,200</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Net contribution: $2,200 less operating cost</td><td>$1,100 / $1.10 per eligible customer</td><td>$1,000 / $1.00 per eligible customer</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The $100 hypothetical difference reverses if integration labor or measured lift changes. Replace every cost with current quotes, including contact or visitor tiers, add-ons, sender costs and encoded message segments. Repeat at peak volume and larger lists; use incremental contribution per eligible customer rather than vendor-attributed revenue per send. Keep the holdout and return window matched.</p>
        <h2>Choose after the workflow passes</h2>
        <p>Choose Privy conditionally when combined capture/email/SMS ownership reduces work and its permission, queue and event tests pass. Choose Justuno conditionally when onsite experiments or personalization solve the bottleneck and the connected sender preserves those controls. An existing email service, including <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a>, or a separate SMS provider is optional: verify the required connection rather than assuming a native integration. A multi-tool stack is justified only when the operating benefit covers its extra handoffs.</p>
        <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint guide</a> to plan the pilot. Record failures and total labor alongside results before changing the production sender.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Justuno FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
